{{also|nèf|nêf}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/nɛf/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|nef}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An extravagant table [[ornament]] and [[container]] used in the [[Middle Ages]] and [[Renaissance]], made in the shape of a [[ship]].

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|cadenas}}

====See also====
* [[navicula]]

===Etymology 2===
Short for ''[[numerically]] [[effective]]''; introduced by Miles Reid.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}
{{wikipedia|Nef line bundle}}

# {{lb|en|algebraic geometry}} Of a [[line bundle]] on a complete algebraic variety over a [[field]]: such that the [[degree]] of its [[restriction]] to every algebraic curve in the [[variety]] is non-negative.
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|title=Minimal Models of Canonical 3-Folds|author=Miles Reid|journal=Advanced Studies in Pure Mathematics|volume=1|year=1983|page=131|passage={{...}} this condition is the numerical consequence of the condition that for some <math>m > 0</math>, the linear system <math>|mD|</math> is effective and free; thus '''nef''' = "numerically (effective and free)".}}

=====Derived terms=====
* [[nefness]]

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=efn|ENF|fen}}

